Feature: A record of what network policy drops
  The cluster audits its network policies, and the decision to enforce them rests on what they would have dropped. Each agent's Hubble holds a few thousand recent flows of every verdict, gone within minutes to hours, so a flow recorder in security-system follows, through Hubble Relay, every flow whose verdict is DROPPED or AUDIT and writes each as one JSON line, which the log collector ships to the log store with every other pod's lines; a recipe reads the store back, counted by workload and port, without an address, and says when the recorder did not run through the window it was asked for. The recorder holds no credential, mounts nothing and reaches Relay and the resolver alone.

  Scenario: The flow recorder is selected by one overlay entry and builds nothing when it is not
    Given the lab-sto2 overlay, which selects the flow recorder with network-audit's ks.yaml beside the namespace directory security-system, and Backstage's catalogue
    When it is built with the entry and without it, and again with Cilium's application left out
    Then with the entry it applies the Kustomization network-audit, which waits and names cilium, and the application builds one Deployment and one CiliumNetworkPolicy in security-system and nothing else; without it the overlay builds the namespace, its default deny and Falco as before and nothing of the recorder; with Cilium left out it is refused, naming network-audit and the missing application; and the catalogue holds the recorder's entity in the system observability, its first link the docs' section on the record and its one dependency Cilium

  Scenario: The flow recorder follows the flows policy drops or would drop through Hubble Relay, one JSON line each that the log collector ships
    Given the network-audit application built as Flux applies it, the render of the Cilium chart with the repository's values, and the log collector's rendered configuration
    When the Deployment's one container, Hubble Relay's Service, pods and configuration, the agents' configuration, and the path of a pod's log lines through the collector are read
    Then the container runs the image the render gives the Cilium agents, tag and digest, a pin Renovate reads under its comment, with the command hubble observe and exactly the flags that name Relay's Service by its name, namespace and port, follow, select the verdicts DROPPED and AUDIT, print jsonpb, and mask each flow to its time, uuid, node name, verdict, drop reason, direction and layer 4 and the namespace, pod name, workloads and labels of its source and of its destination, with no field of a flow's addresses and no other flag; the Deployment has one replica, replaces it before it starts another and counts it available after half a minute, and its pod's TCP keepalive ends a connection that fell silent within a minute and a half; the check fails, saying what the recorder would then need, when Relay's server takes TLS or its Service's port moves; the agents' rendered configuration has Hubble on and reports drops and policy verdicts; and the collector reads every pod's lines, keeps each whole and sends it to the store, the recorder's pod not left out

  Scenario: The flow recorder runs unprivileged, with no token for the API server and nothing of the host
    Given the network-audit application built as Flux applies it and the render of the tree
    When the Deployment's pod, its Pod Security level and the exceptions list are read
    Then the pod passes the restricted level as a non-root user and group with the runtime's seccomp profile, no privilege escalation, every capability dropped and a read-only root filesystem, sets no sysctl but the TCP keepalive's three of its own network namespace, declares no volume at all and mounts no service account token, uses no network, process or IPC namespace of the host and no host port, declares no container port, no probe and no init container, is given five seconds to end, sets requests and a memory limit, and the application builds no Service; the namespace keeps the one exception it had, Falco's, and the check of the render holds the recorder to restricted

