    When the recorder's CiliumNetworkPolicy is read and the flows from and to its pod are evaluated
    Then the policy selects the recorder's pods, leaves the deny to the namespace's default deny, holds no ingress rule and two egress rules, to the resolver on 53 over UDP and TCP and to the pods Relay's Service selects, by its selector's labels in kube-system, on the container port the Service's port targets, and its application names cilium; the recorder reaches Relay on that port and the resolver, and not Relay's other ports, the Service's own port number, another pod of kube-system, the API server, a node's Hubble port, the log store, Prometheus, Falco or the world; nothing reaches it, the local host, Prometheus, the public Gateway's proxies and Relay included; the check of the tree passes; and kube-system's entry in the exceptions list and the docs name the recorder and that port

  Scenario: The audit recipe prints the recorded flows by workload and port, counted, with no address
    Given a copy of the scripts with a kubeconfig of its own and a recording kubectl that answers the store's query with what the pinned store returned for the recorder's lines, in which a world endpoint carries a cidr label with an address and a pod's name a control character, and to which the test adds a field the query does not ask for, with an address
    When just network-audit runs with no argument, with a window and a cluster, and over answers written for the test: with no flow, with no line at all, and with a flow over SCTP, values cut short, labels that name nothing and a port that is an address
    Then it asks the store once, through the API's service proxy, with one query that selects the recorder's lines, unpacks each and has the store count them by verdict, direction, drop reason, the namespace, pod name, workloads and labels of source and destination and the destination port, over the last 24 hours unless told otherwise and for at most 10000 groups, and prints one line per verdict, deciding namespace, direction, source, destination, port and reason with the number of flows, the most frequent first, the groups that differ only by pod name or labels summed, a workload as NAMESPACE/KIND/NAME, a pod without one as NAMESPACE/POD and what is no pod by the name of its reserved label; then, on standard error, how many of the recorder's lines are not flows with the query that shows them, and that the recorder has run since before the window; it exits 0 and prints no address, no other label and no control character, and with no flow says so; and the store's image and its chart's appVersion are the VictoriaLogs release whose answers the recording kubectl gives, and the recorder's image the Cilium release whose hubble printed the lines those answers were made from, both releases recorded in the test's header, so that a store or a recorder of another release fails it, saying to do the store proof again and replace the answers

